From 92ac3e12eb3d82ede5e410a4c7729e4ec37b6705 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bhappe <becker.istvan@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 21:55:42 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Fix four correctness bugs in outcome matching, churn risk and
 campaign wording

All four produced confident but wrong answers on the project's own sample
workbook, and the existing tests missed them because every fixture used
clean "Yes"/"No" labels.

Outcome label matching (utils/helpers.py + four call sites)
  Matching a single exact string counted only one spelling of an outcome as
  positive. On sample_data/marketing_sample.xlsx the Purchased column holds
  seven spellings, so 297 buyers were counted as 14: purchase prediction
  reported 97.4% accuracy for an all-negative model labelled "Very high
  confidence", and the A/B test flipped from p=0.014 to p=0.293. New
  positive_mask() treats affirmative spellings as equivalent when the whole
  column is recognisably yes/no, compares numeric columns directly, and
  otherwise falls back to normalised comparison so unrelated categories are
  never merged. infer_positive_value() now prefers the most common
  affirmative rather than the first in row order.

Churn risk direction (analyses/churn_analysis.py)
  Keywords were matched against an underscored slug, so "dayssince" could
  never match Days_Since_Last_Purchase, which then fell through to the
  risk-decreasing list on the stray word "purchase". The most dormant
  customers scored as the safest: correlation between risk score and days
  since last purchase was -0.52, now +0.51. Keywords now match a compacted
  slug, with recency signals ordered ahead of the decreasing list, and the
  lists cover the value signals that were defaulting to "higher is riskier".

Main risk reason (analyses/churn_analysis.py)
  Picking the largest magnitude regardless of sign named protective factors
  as risk reasons - a customer buying far more often than average was told
  their "purchase frequency has declined". Only contributions above zero can
  be a reason now, and customers with no risk signal are told so. Two related
  defects fixed alongside: top_reasons was counted across all customers
  though the headline describes the high-risk group, and supervised mode
  could reuse a phrase whose assumed direction contradicted the sign the
  model actually learned.

Campaign headline (services/interpretation_service.py)
  better_campaign is always the higher-converting group, but the wording came
  from the sign of the difference, which is measured from the test group. When
  the control group won, the sentence named the winner and said it performed
  lower. The gap is also reported in percentage points rather than "%", with
  both underlying rates quoted and a tie case handled.

Tests: 46 -> 119. New suites cover outcome-label equivalence, churn direction
and reason attribution, and the campaign headline in both directions, each
running against the shipped workbook in its raw uncleaned state. All 40 new
assertions were verified to fail against the previous implementation.

